RI","distance":0.7841,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Nick Rallis"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Rams went 10-7 in 2023 and outscored opponents 404 to 377. The offense carried the team, ranking 9th in EPA per play while the defense finished 22nd in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 11 personnel usage and the 2nd lowest Cover 2 rate in the league.","offense":"The Rams offense was well above average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 11 personnel usage and the most receivers on the field in the league. Matthew Stafford ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Puka Nacua, who drew 29% of the targets.","defense":"The Rams defense was a little below average by EPA allowed per play. Even its strongest area, success rate allowed, only ranked 13th, and it was 29th in takeaways per game. Kobie Turner led the team with 9 sacks, and Byron Young added 8, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd lowest Cover 2 rate in the league.","special":"The Rams were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 74% of field goals, which was one of the worst in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 20% of the money on the books. Their secondary was among the youngest in the league.","players":"Matthew Stafford played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,965 yards. Puka Nacua was the clear top target with 29% of the looks and 1,486 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Kyren Williams and Royce Freeman. On defense, Kobie Turner led the team with 9 sacks and Ahkello Witherspoon picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Sean McVay called the offense as head coach and Raheem Morris called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2025 Jaguars under Liam Coen and the 2025 Cowboys under Brian Schottenheimer. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Chargers under Brandon Staley and the 2024 Panthers under Ejiro Evero.","trends":"The offense went from 29th in EPA per play in 2022 to 9th in 2023, a big step forward. The defense moved from 24th in EPA allowed per play in 2022 to 22nd in 2023, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was pistol usage, which went from 0% to 7%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2023. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}